er bound"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"upoer","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":459.0976310729378,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":328.0976310729378,"r":339.9023689270622,"t":462.9023689270622},"charspan":[0,5],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/15","text":"upoer"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Fo and F1 over the support causes more triangles having positive probability lower bounds; which leads the improvement of my new lower   bound. On the other hand, the Makarov lower bound no such informational because it uses only one triangle while my new lower bound takes advantage of multiple triangles. gets gain","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":323.6666666666667,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.33333333333333,"r":542.3333333333334,"t":397.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,316],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/16","text":"Fo and F1 over the support causes more triangles having positive probability lower bounds; which leads the improvement of my new lower   bound. On the other hand, the Makarov lower bound no such informational because it uses only one triangle while my new lower bound takes advantage of multiple triangles. gets gain"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"5 Application to the Distribution of Effects of Smoking on Birth Weight","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":247.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":72.0,"r":541.6666666666666,"t":297.6666666666667},"charspan":[0,71],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/17","text":"5 Application to the Distribution of Effects of Smoking on Birth Weight"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"In this section; I apply the results presented in Section 3 to an empirical analysis of the distribution of smoking effects on infant birth weight. Smoking not only has direct impact on infant birth weight, but is also associated with unobservable factors that affect infant birth weight. I identify marginal distributions of potential infant birth weight   with without smoking by making use of a state cigarette tax hike in Massachusetts (MA) in January 1993 as a source of exogenous variation. I focus on pregnant women who change their smoking behavior from smoking to nonsmoking in response to the tax increase: To identify the distribution of smoking effects; I impose a MTR restriction that smoking has nonpositive effects on infant birth weight   with probability one. an estimation procedure and report estimates of the DTE and","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":79.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.0,"r":541.6666666666666,"t":232.0},"charspan":[0,836],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/18","text":"In this section; I apply the results presented in Section 3 to an empirical analysis of the distribution of smoking effects on infant birth weight. Smoking not only has direct impact on infant birth weight, but is also associated with unobservable factors that affect infant birth weight. I identify marginal distributions of potential infant birth weight   with without smoking by making use of a state cigarette tax hike in Massachusetts (MA) in January 1993 as a source of exogenous variation. I focus on pregnant women who change their smoking behavior from smoking to nonsmoking in response to the tax increase: To identify the distribution of smoking effects; I impose a MTR restriction that smoking has nonpositive effects on infant birth weight   with probability one. an estimation procedure and report estimates of the DTE and"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"30","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":40.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":300.0,"r":312.6666666666667,"t":50.0},"charspan":[0,2],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/19","text":"30"}],"version":"1.10.0"}